itle-size);font-weight:var(--ds-font-title-weight)\">Learning objectives</h2><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:var(--ds-space-lg);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--ds-space-sm);font-size:var(--ds-font-body-size);line-height:var(--ds-font-body-line-height)\"><li>Explain how shared electron pairs form covalent bonds.</li><li>Draw Lewis structures for simple molecules such as H2O and CO2.</li><li>Compare single, double and triple bonds by bond length and strength.</li></ul></section><section class=\"card\"><h2 style=\"margin:0 0 var(--ds-space-sm);font-family:var(--ds-font-title-family);font-size:var(--ds-font-title-size);font-weight:var(--ds-font-title-weight)\">Key idea</h2><p style=\"margin:0;font-size:var(--ds-font-body-size);line-height:var(--ds-font-body-line-height)\">In a covalent bond, two atoms share one or more pairs of electrons so both can reach a stable outer shell. The shared pair is attracted to both nuclei, holding the atoms together.</p><div style=\"margin-top:var(--ds-space-md);border:1px dashed var(--ds-color-border);border-radius:var(--ds-radius-md);padding:var(--ds-space-lg);display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;min-height:160px;color:var(--ds-color-text-muted);font-size:var(--ds-font-label-size)\">Placeholder: diagram of shared electron pair</div></section><div style=\"display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:var(--ds-space-md)\"><button class=\"btn btn-secondary\">Previous lesson</button><button class=\"btn btn-primary\">Mark complete &amp; continue</button></div></main><aside style=\"border-left:1px solid var(--ds-color-border);padding:var(--ds-space-lg);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--ds-space-lg);overflow:auto\"><section class=\"card\"><h2 style=\"margin:0 0 var(--ds-space-sm);font-family:var(--ds-font-label-family);font-size:var(--ds-font-label-size);font-weight:var(--ds-font-label-weight);text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.05em;color:var(--ds-color-text-muted)\">Your progress</h2><span st